1 hus, on this account again, we had to pay out in the second five-year period over $65,000,000 more than the previous administration during the first five-year period. In a word, the present government paid off 190 millions of debt during the last five years, and had to pay out an extra 141 millions in interest and an extra 65 millions in pension charges, as compared with those paid in the previous five years. It will be readily seen that the ordinary administration of the country is being carried on vastly more economically in the second five-year period than in. the first.
My hon. friend asks, where is the reduction in taxation under the present administration? He must have forgotten all the reductions that have taken place. May I refresh his memory with respect to some of them at least. Here are some:	In regard to the income tax, the following exemptions and reductions have been made:
1922-Exemption for each child raised from $200 to $300;
1924-Additional exemption for each child raised from $300 to $500;
1926-	Exemption limit raised from $2,000 to $3,000 in the case of married persons or those with dependents and from $1,000 to $1,500 in the case of other persons, in addition those with incomes of $5,000 or less to pay only 2 per cent of their taxable income instead of 4 per cent. Corporation income tax reduced from 10 per cent to 9 per cent;
1927-	10 per cent cut off the 1926 rates;
1928-	Additional 10 per cent cut off the 1926 rates.
Now I come to the tariff changes. These are as follows:
1922-	Eeductions on sugar, agricultural implements, textiles, boots and shoes, etc.;
1923-	Tariff on goods imported under the British preferential tariff reduced by 10 per cent wherever regular rate of duty exceeds 15 per cent and where goods are imported directly to a sea or river port of Canada. Duty on sugar reduced;
1924-	Duty on instruments of production used in agriculture, mining, forestry and fisheries reduced;

The Budget-Mr. Mackenzie King
1925-	Reductions on well-drilling machinery and engines for fishermen's boats;
1926-	Duties on sugar, automobiles, tin plate, etc., reduced;
1928-Numerous changes contained in Chapter 17 of the statutes for 1928.
I put on Hansard last year at page 1301 of Volume II and page following, a more or less complete statement of the reductions in the tariff since the present government came into office up to 1928.
As to postage, we restored penny postage within Canada July 1, 1926, and in 1928 imperial penny postage was reestablished.
The following statement shows the reductions we have made in the cheque tax, the receipt tax and the sales tax:
Cheque Tax
1922-	Imposed at 2c. on every $50 with a maximum of $2 on $5,000 or over;
1923-	Reduced to $1;
1925-	Tax on cheques not exceeding $5 remitted ;
1927-	Tax reduced to a flat 2c. on cheques of over $10;
Receipt Tax
1926-	Imposed in 1922; abolished in 1926.
Sales Tax
1923-	A flat 6 per cent;
1924-	Reduced to 5 per cent;
1927-	Reduced to 4 per cent;
1928-	Reduced to 3 per cent;
1929-	Reduced to 2 per cent.
A list of the earlier changes made by the government in customs duties will be found at pages 2137 to 2141 of revised Hansard of May 15, 1924.
This .government, to state the fact, has been making a continuous reduction in taxation year by year. Statements have been made by the Minister of Finance in his different budgets indicative of the amounts of taxation that have been remitted. In 1926 the estimated reduction of taxation was $25,000,000; in 1927, $27,000,000; in 1928, $19,000,000; in 1929-the present year-$25,000,000. That is, within the last four years there has been an estimated reduction in taxation of almost $100,000,000. A reduction, be it remembered, which constitutes from now on an annual reduction as compared with 1926.
That is the record of the present administration with respect to taxation.
I come, Mi. Speaker, to another subject discussed by my hon. friend, and to a phase of that subject which has been taken up at some length in the course of the debate by hon. gentlemen opposite. Now that the Canadian National Railways have begun to pay,
now that everyone recognizes that the Canadian National system is an asset to the country, and that the country is profiting by it in the eyes of the world., hon. gentlemen apposite are seeking to claim the paternity of that institution. Hon. gentlemen opposite are desirous of having it appear that they are responsible for the Canadian National system being what it is to-day. My hon. friend used all the skill of which he was capable in seeking to make out a case to that end. He started out, by saying that with respect to the national railways they on the opposite side were all optimists. Well, it is easy to be an optimist when anything is going well, and particularly where its success has been assured by someone else. But my hon. friends were not always optimists. They were not optimists even in 1921 in their last year of office and when appealing to the country. I recall very well the discussions that took place at that time. The most they said about the railways was that Sir Wilfrid Laurier was responsible for the condition they were then in. It was stated that Sir Wilfrid Laurier was responsible for having constructed the Grand Trank Pacific, for having built the National Transcontinental, for having been a friend of Mackenzie and Mann; that was the kind of thing one heard throughout the country as late as 1921 when hon. gentlemen opposite were hoping they might be returned to power.
But, as I have said, my bon. friend, in the course of the present debate, sought to build up a case to have it appear that at the time these railway matters were up for consideration in parliament, the Liberals were doing their utmost to destroy tihe Canadian National system. He said he well remembered the time that parliament sat in the old Victoria museum, in the fall of 1919. He was kind enough to remind me that in that year I came back into the bouse as leader of the Liberal party. He went on to say my first act was an endeavour to destroy these veiy railways for the success of which the government is now claiming so much credit. He said he recalled the discussion very well, that Hon. D. D. Mackenzie, as leader of the Liberal party in the early part of the session, had first of all sought to postpone any consideration of the question of the consolidation of the national railways, any organization of the Canadian National Railway system: that he was followed later by Mr. Fielding, who suggested that a royal commission should be appointed to inquire into the matter, but that the real intention was to destroy the organization of the Canadian National system. Then he said that I had brought in a motion to Tefer the whole matter back to
